%=============================================================================
\section{Summary}
%=============================================================================

This chapter has developed the framework for analyzing and specifying control system performance in both transient and steady-state regimes.

\subsection*{Key Concepts}

\textbf{Transient Response Specifications}:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Rise time} ($t_r$): Measures response speed; primarily determined by $\omega_n$
    \item \textbf{Peak time} ($t_p$): Time to first overshoot; $t_p = \pi/\omega_d$
    \item \textbf{Percent overshoot} (PO): Depends only on $\zeta$; lower $\zeta$ means more overshoot
    \item \textbf{Settling time} ($t_s$): Time to reach steady state; $t_s \approx 4/(\zeta\omega_n)$
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Steady-State Error Analysis}:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{System type}: Number of integrators in open-loop transfer function
    \item \textbf{Error constants} ($K_p$, $K_v$, $K_a$): Determine error magnitude for polynomial inputs
    \item Type 0: Finite step error, infinite ramp error
    \item Type 1: Zero step error, finite ramp error
    \item Type 2: Zero step and ramp error, finite parabolic error
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Fundamental Tradeoffs}:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Higher gain reduces steady-state error but often increases overshoot
    \item Faster response (higher $\omega_n$) requires higher gain
    \item Adding integrators eliminates steady-state error but reduces stability margins
    \item The s-plane provides a geometric framework for balancing multiple specifications
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Design Guidelines}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Begin by determining required $\zeta$ from overshoot specification
    \item Calculate required $\sigma = \zeta\omega_n$ from settling time specification
    \item Determine minimum $\omega_n$ from the ratio $\sigma/\zeta$
    \item Check steady-state error requirements; add integrators or increase gain as needed
    \item Verify that stability margins remain adequate
    \item Use compensation (lead, lag, or PID) to satisfy conflicting requirements
\end{enumerate}

%=============================================================================
\section*{Problems}
%=============================================================================

\begin{enumerate}
    \item A second-order system has poles at $s = -2 \pm j3$. Calculate:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item Natural frequency $\omega_n$ and damping ratio $\zeta$
        \item Rise time, peak time, percent overshoot, and settling time
        \item The closed-loop transfer function (assuming unity DC gain)
    \end{enumerate}

    \item For the system $G(s) = \dfrac{20(s+3)}{s^2(s+5)(s+10)}$:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item Determine the system type
        \item Calculate all error constants
        \item Find the steady-state error for inputs $r(t) = 5$, $r(t) = 2t$, and $r(t) = t^2$
    \end{enumerate}

    \item Design a second-order system to achieve:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Settling time $\leq 0.5$ s
        \item Percent overshoot $\leq 5\%$
    \end{itemize}
    Specify $\zeta$, $\omega_n$, and pole locations.

    \item A unity feedback system has $G(s) = \dfrac{K}{s+10}$.
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item What is the system type?
        \item Find $K$ for 5\% steady-state error to a step input
        \item With this $K$, what is the closed-loop time constant?
    \end{enumerate}

    \item For the motor control experiment, if the measured step response shows:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Rise time: 0.15 s
        \item Overshoot: 25\%
        \item Settling time: 0.8 s
    \end{itemize}
    Estimate the effective $\zeta$ and $\omega_n$ of the closed-loop system.

    \item A Type 1 system must track a ramp input with velocity 10 units/s with steady-state error less than 0.1 units. What minimum velocity error constant $K_v$ is required?

    \item Sketch the allowable region in the s-plane for dominant poles that satisfy:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Settling time $\leq 2$ s
        \item Overshoot $\leq 15\%$
        \item Rise time $\leq 0.5$ s
    \end{itemize}

    \item Explain why adding an integrator to improve steady-state error may degrade transient performance. What compensation technique can restore good transient response?

    \item A hard disk servo must position the head within 0.1 $\mu$m of the target track within 4 ms. If the maximum acceptable overshoot is 2\%, estimate the required $\zeta$ and $\omega_n$.

    \item Compare the step responses of three systems, all with $\omega_n = 5$ rad/s:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $\zeta = 0.3$
        \item $\zeta = 0.7$
        \item $\zeta = 1.5$
    \end{enumerate}
    Calculate and tabulate all transient specifications. Which provides the best compromise between speed and smoothness?
\end{enumerate}

%=============================================================================
\section*{Further Reading}
%=============================================================================

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Ziegler, J.G., and Nichols, N.B., ``Optimum Settings for Automatic Controllers,'' \textit{Transactions of the ASME}, Vol.\ 64, pp.\ 759--768, 1942. (The foundational paper on performance-based tuning.)

    \item James, H.M., Nichols, N.B., and Phillips, R.S., \textit{Theory of Servomechanisms}, McGraw-Hill, 1947. (The MIT Radiation Laboratory's wartime research, declassified and published.)

    \item Franklin, G.F., Powell, J.D., and Emami-Naeini, A., \textit{Feedback Control of Dynamic Systems}, 8th ed., Pearson, 2019. (Comprehensive modern treatment of transient and steady-state analysis.)

    \item Ogata, K., \textit{Modern Control Engineering}, 5th ed., Prentice Hall, 2010. (Clear derivations of all second-order system formulas.)

    \item Bennett, S., \textit{A History of Control Engineering}, Peter Peregrinus, 1993. (Excellent historical context for the development of performance specifications.)
\end{enumerate}

